            <ArticleTitle>Coxiella burnetii Lipopolysaccharide: What Do We Know?</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">E2509</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3390/ijms18122509</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A small gram-negative bacterium, Coxiella burnetii (C. burnetii), is responsible for a zoonosis called Q fever. C. burnetii is an intracellular bacterium that can survive inside microbicidal cells like monocytes and macrophages by hijacking several functions of the immune system. Among several virulence factors, the lipopolysaccharide (LPS) of C. burnetii is one of the major factors involved in this immune hijacking because of its atypical composition and structure. Thus, the aim of this mini-review is to summarize the repressive effects of C. burnetii LPS on the antibacterial immunity of cells.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
